# Source: Lecture Quiz 2 (solutions), third question (the variable "fact" is renamed f).
id: factorial-wrong
title: A factorial loop with a bug
topic: Loops
level: 3
source: Lecture Quiz 2, factorial
tags: while, invariant, counterexample, product

task:
  The program below should compute the factorial of $n$. The postcondition
  uses $prod(k, 1, n, k) = 1 * 2 * ... * n$. The invariant
  $1 <= i <= n && f = prod(k, 1, i, k)$ is proposed for the loop.

  Check the proposed invariant, and decide whether the triple is valid.
  Choose the invariant above the steps: the first one is the invariant
  that the loop really keeps, and it leads to the verdict; the second one
  is the proposed invariant, and its proof fails.

given:
  Loop condition B: $i < n$

pre: i = 1 && f = 1 && n >= 1
post: f = prod(k, 1, n, k)

program:
  {P}
  {I}
  while (i < n) do {
    {I && i < n}
    {R2}
    f := f * i;      // b1
    {R1}
    i := i + 1       // b2
    {I}
  }
  {I && !(i < n)}
  {Q}

verdict: invalid

choice: I: 1 ≤ i ≤ n ∧ f = prod(k, 1, i − 1, k)
outcome: proves

step: The invariant I
rule: WHILE-CONSEQUENCE
focus: while
text:
  We trace the loop from $i = 1$, $f = 1$:

  | Loop head | $i$ | $f$ |
  |---|---|---|
  | before iteration 1 | $1$ | $1$ |
  | before iteration 2 | $2$ | $1$ |
  | before iteration 3 | $3$ | $2$ |
  | before iteration 4 | $4$ | $6$ |

  The body multiplies $f$ by $i$ before it increases $i$, so $f$ is the
  product of the numbers below $i$: $f = 1 * 2 * ... * (i - 1)$, and not
  the product up to $i$ as proposed in the task. We keep the bounds of the
  proposed invariant.
set: I = 1 <= i <= n && f = prod(k, 1, i - 1, k)
text:
  The loop rule with consequence has three premises, with the loop
  condition $B$ being $i < n$: initialisation $P ==> I$, preservation of
  $I$ by the body, and conclusion $I && !B ==> Q$.

step: P implies I
part: Initialisation
rule: CONSEQUENCE, LOGIC
focus: P, I
text:
  The invariant must hold when the loop is reached. No statement comes
  before the loop, so the precondition $P$ must imply $I$.
  >>> «P» ==> «I»
vc: i = 1 && f = 1 && n >= 1 ==> 1 <= i <= n && f = prod(k, 1, i - 1, k)
why:
  With $i = 1$ the product $prod(k, 1, 0, k)$ has no term, so it is $1$,
  which is $f$. The bounds $1 <= 1 <= n$ follow from $n >= 1$.

step: R1, the weakest precondition of i := i + 1 for I
part: Preservation
rule: ASSIGN, WP
focus: b2
text:
  One iteration must keep the invariant:
  >>> {«I» && «i < n : B»} f := f * i; i := i + 1 {«I»}
  We calculate the weakest precondition of the body for $I$, from the last
  statement. We substitute $i$ with $i + 1$ in $I$.
calc:
  wp(i := i + 1, I)
  = (1 <= i <= n && f = prod(k, 1, i - 1, k))[i <- i + 1]     // I
  = 1 <= i + 1 <= n && f = prod(k, 1, i + 1 - 1, k)          // i is replaced by i + 1
  = 1 <= i + 1 <= n && f = prod(k, 1, i, k)                  // i + 1 - 1 is i
  = 0 <= i <= n - 1 && f = prod(k, 1, i, k)                  // subtract 1 from each part of the bounds
set: R1 = 0 <= i <= n - 1 && f = prod(k, 1, i, k)

step: R2, the weakest precondition of f := f * i for R1
part: Preservation
rule: ASSIGN, WP
focus: b1
text:
  We substitute $f$ with $f * i$ in $R1$, computed in the previous step.
calc:
  wp(f := f * i, R1)
  = (0 <= i <= n - 1 && f = prod(k, 1, i, k))[f <- f * i]     // R1
  = 0 <= i <= n - 1 && f * i = prod(k, 1, i, k)              // f is replaced by f * i
set: R2 = 0 <= i <= n - 1 && f * i = prod(k, 1, i, k)

step: I and B imply R2
part: Preservation
rule: CONSEQUENCE, LOGIC
focus: I && i < n, R2
text:
  By the consequence rule, the triple holds when $I && B$ implies $R2$.
  >>> «I» && «i < n : B» ==> «R2»
vc: 1 <= i <= n && f = prod(k, 1, i - 1, k) && i < n ==> 0 <= i <= n - 1 && f * i = prod(k, 1, i, k)
why:
  From $1 <= i$ we get $0 <= i$, and for integers $i < n$ is $i <= n - 1$.
  Since $i >= 1$, $prod(k, 1, i, k) = prod(k, 1, i - 1, k) * i$, which is
  $f * i$ by $I$.

step: I and not B do not imply Q
part: Conclusion
rule: CONSEQUENCE, LOGIC
focus: I && !(i < n), Q
text:
  When the loop stops, $I$ holds and $B$ is false. This must imply the
  postcondition $Q$.
  >>> «I» && «!(i < n) : ¬B» ==> «Q»
vc: 1 <= i <= n && f = prod(k, 1, i - 1, k) && !(i < n) ==> f = prod(k, 1, n, k)
status: fails
counterexample: i = 2, f = 1, n = 2
why:
  $!(i < n)$ and $i <= n$ give $i = n$, so $I$ gives
  $f = 1 * ... * (n - 1)$, which is not $1 * ... * n$ when $n >= 2$.
text:
  A failed premise shows only that this invariant does not prove the
  triple. Here the state $i = 2$, $f = 1$, $n = 2$ is reached by the
  program: from $i = 1$, $f = 1$, $n = 2$ the loop runs once and stops. So
  the triple itself is not valid.
vc: {i = 1 && f = 1 && n >= 1} while (i < n) do { f := f * i; i := i + 1 } {f = prod(k, 1, n, k)}
status: fails
counterexample: i = 1, f = 1, n = 2
why:
  From this state the program ends with $i = 2$ and $f = 1$, but
  $prod(k, 1, 2, k) = 2$.
result: The triple is not valid: the program computes $(n - 1)!$ instead of $n!$.

choice: Proposed I: 1 ≤ i ≤ n ∧ f = prod(k, 1, i, k)
outcome: fails

step: The proposed invariant I
rule: WHILE-CONSEQUENCE
focus: while
text:
  We take the invariant proposed in the task.
set: I = 1 <= i <= n && f = prod(k, 1, i, k)
text:
  The loop rule with consequence has three premises, with the loop
  condition $B$ being $i < n$: initialisation $P ==> I$, preservation of
  $I$ by the body, and conclusion $I && !B ==> Q$.

step: P implies I
part: Initialisation
rule: CONSEQUENCE, LOGIC
focus: P, I
text:
  The invariant must hold when the loop is reached. No statement comes
  before the loop, so the precondition $P$ must imply $I$.
  >>> «P» ==> «I»
vc: i = 1 && f = 1 && n >= 1 ==> 1 <= i <= n && f = prod(k, 1, i, k)
why:
  With $i = 1$: $1 <= 1 <= n$ follows from $n >= 1$, and
  $prod(k, 1, 1, k) = 1 = f$.

step: R1, the weakest precondition of i := i + 1 for I
part: Preservation
rule: ASSIGN, WP
focus: b2
text:
  One iteration must keep the invariant:
  >>> {«I» && «i < n : B»} f := f * i; i := i + 1 {«I»}
  We calculate the weakest precondition of the body for $I$, from the last
  statement. We substitute $i$ with $i + 1$ in $I$.
calc:
  wp(i := i + 1, I)
  = (1 <= i <= n && f = prod(k, 1, i, k))[i <- i + 1]     // I
  = 1 <= i + 1 <= n && f = prod(k, 1, i + 1, k)          // i is replaced by i + 1
  = 0 <= i <= n - 1 && f = prod(k, 1, i + 1, k)          // subtract 1 from each part of the bounds
set: R1 = 0 <= i <= n - 1 && f = prod(k, 1, i + 1, k)

step: R2, the weakest precondition of f := f * i for R1
part: Preservation
rule: ASSIGN, WP
focus: b1
text:
  We substitute $f$ with $f * i$ in $R1$, computed in the previous step.
calc:
  wp(f := f * i, R1)
  = (0 <= i <= n - 1 && f = prod(k, 1, i + 1, k))[f <- f * i]     // R1
  = 0 <= i <= n - 1 && f * i = prod(k, 1, i + 1, k)              // f is replaced by f * i
set: R2 = 0 <= i <= n - 1 && f * i = prod(k, 1, i + 1, k)

step: I and B do not imply R2
part: Preservation
rule: CONSEQUENCE, LOGIC
focus: I && i < n, R2
text:
  By the consequence rule, the triple holds when $I && B$ implies $R2$.
  >>> «I» && «i < n : B» ==> «R2»
vc: 1 <= i <= n && f = prod(k, 1, i, k) && i < n ==> 0 <= i <= n - 1 && f * i = prod(k, 1, i + 1, k)
status: fails
counterexample: i = 1, f = 1, n = 2
why:
  From $f = 1 * ... * i$ the body computes $f * i = 1 * ... * i * i$, but
  $R2$ needs $1 * ... * i * (i + 1)$. In the state $i = 1$, $f = 1$,
  $n = 2$: $f * i = 1$, and $prod(k, 1, 2, k) = 2$. So the proposed
  formula is not an invariant of the loop.

step: I and not B imply Q
part: Conclusion
rule: CONSEQUENCE, LOGIC
focus: I && !(i < n), Q
text:
  When the loop stops, $I$ holds and $B$ is false. This must imply the
  postcondition $Q$.
  >>> «I» && «!(i < n) : ¬B» ==> «Q»
vc: 1 <= i <= n && f = prod(k, 1, i, k) && !(i < n) ==> f = prod(k, 1, n, k)
why:
  $!(i < n)$ is $i >= n$. Together with $i <= n$ it gives $i = n$, so
  $f = prod(k, 1, i, k)$ becomes $f = prod(k, 1, n, k)$. This premise
  holds, but the proof fails because preservation fails: the proposed
  formula would give the postcondition if the loop kept it, and the loop
  does not keep it.

summary:
  The failed preservation premise of the proposed invariant was a sign of
  a bug in the program. Swapping the two statements of the body,
  `i := i + 1; f := f * i`, gives a correct program, for which
  $1 <= i <= n && f = prod(k, 1, i, k)$ is an invariant.
